{# DECIDED: prometheus-callers sits in the observability namespace and admits callers from
   the homelab namespace and from flux-system, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   k8s/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape.
#}
---
# prometheus is one of three observability workloads (with loki and alertmanager) that need a
# per-workload grant for an in-cluster, cross-namespace caller. Five callers are admitted:
#   - monitor-bridge and homelab-mcp (PROMETHEUS_URL), and homepage (the Headlamp tile's
#     customapi widget URL, k8s/roles/homepage/templates/config/services.yaml.j2), all in
#     {{ k8s_namespace }}.
#   - crowdsec's verify Job, `app: crowdsec-verify` (k8s/roles/crowdsec/templates/verify/
#     job.yaml.j2), in {{ k8s_namespace }}. It reads the edge's answers to daniel-pi's probe.
#   - the soak Job in flux-system, which reads `homelab:pod_service:info` and the critical alerts
#     to decide whether a promotion is healthy. Its name moves with each revision's SHA, so the
#     peer selects the stable pod label `app: soak` instead of `job-name`.
#
# prometheus is ALSO read on host loopback by `otelq` (a workstation host process — see
# observability/CLAUDE.md), covered the same way as tempo/otel-collector below, not by this
# file. Headlamp's Prometheus plugin is another in-cluster reader this file does NOT admit, on
# purpose: it queries through the API server's service proxy
# (k8s/roles/headlamp/templates/rbac.yaml.j2), so the hop that reaches the pod is the API
# server's, from the node's cni0/flannel.1 address — the same node-CIDR peer that carries otelq.
# The other four observability workloads need no per-workload policy, each for a different
# reason — don't collapse them into one mechanism:
#   - tempo, otel-collector: loopback-hostPort callers only, admitted by the node-CIDR `ipBlock`
#     peer (`netpol_baseline_obs_node_cidrs`, networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2), which carries
#     no `ports` restriction — trimming that list to "nameable" entries breaks otelq silently.
#   - grafana: Traefik only, admitted by that same file's explicit traefik
#     namespaceSelector+podSelector peer — unrelated to the ipBlock/CIDR list above.
#   - kube-state-metrics: no external caller at all; prometheus scrapes it from inside the
#     namespace, covered by the plain `podSelector: {}` peer.
#
# INGRESS ONLY, same as the rest of this role: an Egress policy selects pods correctly on this
# cluster and blocks nothing. Writing one would read like a control in the repo and do nothing in
# the cluster.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prometheus-callers
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: prometheus
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool`, matching `netpol_baseline_probe_active` in defaults/main.yml: a STRING value like
   `'false'` is truthy to bare Jinja and False to that list, which picks the verify Job's legs,
   so without `| bool` the two sides disagree and this policy would fence prometheus with no
   probe to catch it. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_obs_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        # namespaceSelector and podSelector are SIBLING KEYS in ONE list item, so they AND
        # together: one of the named apps, but only inside {{ k8s_namespace }}. As two
        # separate list items they would OR, admitting the entire {{ k8s_namespace }} namespace
        # (or any pod anywhere carrying one of these labels) rather than just these four
        # callers. The soak peer below follows the same rule.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ k8s_namespace }}
          podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values: [monitor-bridge, homelab-mcp, homepage, crowdsec-verify]
        # The soak Job, as ONE item for the same reason: `app: soak`, but only inside
        # flux-system. Split in two, the namespaceSelector alone would admit every pod in
        # flux-system. The Job template sets the label on its POD template, because a
        # NetworkPolicy matches pod labels and never the Job object's own.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: flux-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: soak
      # Port-9090-wide is the finest an L3/L4 NetworkPolicy can express: it does not distinguish a
      # read (query) from a write/admin call on the same port. That is only safe because
      # Prometheus runs without --web.enable-admin-api, --web.enable-lifecycle and
      # --web.enable-remote-write-receiver (all absent from observability/prometheus.yaml.j2).
      # Adding any of those flags silently turns this grant into a write/admin grant to all five
      # callers with no NetworkPolicy-side change to flag in review. homepage matters most: its
      # widget proxy fetches whatever URL its rendered config names, so its reach here is only as
      # narrow as services.yaml.j2.
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 9090
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
